Singapore’s tourism sector is experiencing a strong recovery with inbound tourists up 19 times compared to 2021.
(Singapore Tourism Board announces that international visitors to Singapore in 2023 will number between 12 million and 14 million, generating approximately S$18 billion to S$21 billion in tourism revenue; visitor numbers and tourism revenues will return to around 67-75% of pre-COVID levels in 2019; there is great likelihood for full recovery of the tourism sector in 2024.)
The Singapore tourism market gradually reopens and recovers in 2022.
The latest statistics released by the Singapore Tourism Board showed that the number of tourists entering the country in 2022 increased 19 times from 330,600 in 2021 to 6.3 million, exceeding the earlier forecast of 4 million to 6 million, and recovered to 33% of the pre-pandemic level.
From January to September 2022, Singapore tourism receipts amounted to S$8.96 billion, and it is expected that the tourism receipts for the whole year will reach S$13.8 billion to S$14.3 billion, which is 6 to 7 times higher than S$1.89 billion in 2021, and have recovered to about 50% to 52% of the level before the COVID-19 pandemic.
The Singapore government has taken many steps to accelerate the recovery of tourism.
In April 2022, the government announced that it would allocate around $490 million to stimulate tourism recovery.
The tourism board announced that it plans to allocate 110 million yuan from the budget over the next two years to launch more business and leisure activities to attract returning tourists.
To make health and wellness a tourist attraction in Singapore, the Singapore Tourism Board launched an expression of interest exercise on 23 November 2022 to develop a health and wellness site along the southern coast of Singapore.
In recent years, more and more tourists have put their health first among all considerations, said Wang Linli, executive director of sports and wellness tourism at the Singapore Tourism Board.
The development of a health and wellness resort in the Hainan district of Singapore, which has the Hanging Bay Gardens and Hanging Bay Dams, is expected to contribute to Singapore's tourism and economy, allowing tourists to spend and stay longer in Singapore.
Singapore Tourism Board Director Chen Jianlong said that in the coming years, healthy living will be an important part of Singapore's tourism product.
The coronavirus outbreak has raised public awareness and demand for physical and mental health.
For this reason, the Tourism Board has been promoting health and wellness programs to the local public and international tourists for the past two years.
In June 2022, the Singapore Tourism Board held the first Health and Wellness Festival, providing more than 130 different types of health activities and experiences for the public.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences to visitors.
In addition to the new Singapore Children's Museum, the Gardens by the Bay's Avatar World, the ArtScience Museum's "Explore New Frontiers" exhibition hall, and the Night Safari's new outdoor theater, the Tourism Board will support the development and upgrading of Wildlife Safari's Feather Park and other projects, making Singapore an attractive international travel destination.
As planned, Singapore will develop relevant industry standards for sustainable tourism conferences, exhibitions and awards by 2023, focusing on improving both waste management and carbon emissions, further optimizing the tourism environment.
The recovery of Singapore's tourism industry has accelerated the recovery of the aviation industry.
Data shows that as of December 18, 2022, Changi Airport has seen a strong recovery in passenger traffic, with daily passengers reaching over 150,000 and weekly numbers around 1 million, equivalent to about 75% of pre-pandemic levels, and the number of employees back at work is also up to approximately 90% of their pre-pandemic level.
Data showed that from January to October this year, Singapore received 23.6 million tourists, which is equivalent to 42% of the pre-epidemic level, and completed 170,000 flights, which is equivalent to 53% of the pre-epidemic level.
Tourism’s rapid rebound has also spurred recovery in sectors such as accommodation, transportation, dining, and shopping.
The Singapore government and the tourism industry are optimistic about the Chinese market.
Tourism Board’s Assistant Director for International Affairs Koh Shudan said that a survey of China travel platforms revealed Singapore’s safety index to be among the top three globally, which is also one reason why Singapore enjoys popularity with Chinese tourists.
Before the outbreak, about 3.6 million Chinese people visited Singapore for holidays every year.
With China's border liberalization, Chinese tourism is expected to return to 30% to 60% in 2019 this year.
Ko Shudan noted that the Tourism Bureau will closely monitor signals from China on further liberalization of outbound tourism policies to respond to changes in tourist demand.
In addition to strengthening partnerships with Chinese online travel platforms such as Travel Travel, the Singapore Tourism Board will be actively preparing for the new trends in overseas travel in China.
Looking ahead to the 2023 tourism market, the Singapore Tourism Authority is confident that the recovery in the tourism sector will be faster than expected as long as there is no significant recession in the global economy affecting public revenues and no new large-scale outbreaks of deadly outbreaks.
A quick guide to internet finance
This book is very shallow; it can be considered an introductory course that will help you quickly understand the broad picture of internet finance but to really delve deeper, you need to follow the map and dig on your own.
And it was written earlier, and the content of the later WeChat and Alipay subsidy war to cultivate user habits has not been mentioned. The proportion of third-party payment is probably not as small as it is mentioned in the book now.
Nowadays, many people take their phones out to eat instead of carrying cash, and banks have also abandoned UnionPay due to high bank transaction volumes.
But still, I am very grateful to the author for writing this introductory book; it is evident that the author has had many years of financial observation experience and is familiar with both domestic and international financial development histories, which allows him/her to write in such an easy-to-understand manner.
Writing highbrow is easy; making it accessible is hard.
