August 14th I just got the German version of DN1080. The exterior workmanship is so bad.
1. The front panel and the knob are made of plastic.
2. Poor audio inputs on the rear panel and speaker output jacks; any CD amp from $100 to $200 is much better than this one.
3. The top cover is relatively thin, and the transformer side is sunken in, so I had to open the box and use my hand to restore it. The top cover and the front panel are loose.
4. The machine's center of gravity leans towards the transformer end, making it awkward to move.
It feels like an entry-level Sony machine with that build quality; Malaysian made.
It seems Sony would be better off manufacturing in China than with Xiaoma.
The machine is heated, the two-way radio used in the cinema for about 2 hours, the top cover is touched with the hand at 40-50 ° C, do not know if the transformer is too hot.
Nowadays, most use switch-mode power supplies; this big transformer must weigh at least 4 kg.
Sony should have a silent fan with active heat in the machine, and when the box is opened, only the large silicon heat sink on the tube will be visible.
I feel bad.
Firstly, the box is not very sturdy; it deforms when pressed down once. Secondly, the design on the backside does not prevent ABS from scratching the surface of the box when placed flat, and it also fails to achieve its intended purpose. Thirdly, the inside of the box is just a cloth bag with a handle, which stands out prominently. All these various dissatisfaction culminates in the zipper being slightly difficult to operate.
